 ## Overview
-Two-stage NeRF Rendering is an acceleration technique for Neural Radiance Fields that decouples the estimation of scene density from the detailed evaluation of color and density. It solves the problem of high computational cost in volume rendering by first using a coarse pass to identify high-density regions, then using those results to importance-sample a fine pass [S1][S2, unofficial][S3]. It sets itself apart from uniform sampling by concentrating computational effort on relevant geometry, and from single-stage methods by explicitly separating the rough density estimation from the detailed evaluation [S2, unofficial][S3].
+Two-stage NeRF Rendering is an acceleration technique for Neural Radiance Fields that decouples scene density estimation from detailed color evaluation [S1]. It reduces computational cost by first using a coarse pass to identify high-density regions, then importance-sampling a fine pass to refine the result [S1][S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from uniform sampling by concentrating computational effort on relevant geometry, and from single-stage methods by explicitly separating rough density estimation from detailed evaluation [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method consists of two distinct rendering passes: a coarse pass to estimate the transmittance and density distribution along rays, and a fine pass that refines the color and density estimates using samples concentrated in high-density regions identified by the coarse pass.
